CAVEAT:  Two limits on what this run proved.
         1. NOT MEASURED -- whether the client recovers by itself after a manual
            `clock set`. Step 4 shows that a running chronyd stays unsynchronised after
            the clock is moved by hand, while it keeps polling successfully and keeps
            reporting the true offset. The DUT had NOT recovered within the two windows
            this run allowed: 90 s at an offset of 3523 s, and a further 75 s at an offset
            of 2.12 s. Whether chronyd would slew the clock back after a longer time is
            NOT MEASURED. At a maximum slew rate of 500 ppm, 2.12 s would need about
            70 minutes, and nothing in this run separates "the daemon refuses" from "the
            daemon is slewing too slowly to see". What IS measured is that the device sat
            at `Not synchronised` for both windows with a healthy, fully reachable server,
            and that restarting the client steps the clock in 2 s.
         2. This case tested the plain client role only. It did not test NTP
            authentication keys, `ntp peer`, `ntp pool`, the `prefer` keyword, a VRF, or
            more than one reachable server at a time.
