03:53:03.201933 v4 10.38.22.34:37753 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=10:31:57.093908
03:53:05.214598 v4 10.38.22.34:54407 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=19:03:59.702545
03:54:10.147907 v4 10.38.22.34:50059 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=16:54:51.655083
03:55:14.834618 v4 10.38.22.34:55646 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=10:15:47.715640
03:56:19.438462 v4 10.38.22.34:44203 -> 10.38.22.33:123  client ... xmit=18:31:21.863853
  (each with its server reply)

  29 frames in the capture, 14 NTP packets on port 123
  by mode:   client(3)=7  server(4)=7
  10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33   7
  10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34   7
  pairing:   requests=7 replies=7  paired=7 unpaired=0 unanswered=0

  Reading it:
   - 7 polls, 7 replies, 7 paired, 0 unanswered to the reachable server. That count
     matches `show ntp counters`'s `Client Valid Received 7` exactly. The device's own
     counter and the wire agree.
   - ZERO NTP packets went to 10.38.22.40. The control did not merely fail to
     synchronise. The DUT never even sent it an NTP packet. The capture filter was
     `udp port 123 or arp host 10.38.22.40`, and of the 29 frames only 14 were NTP, so
     the other 15 are the DUT's unanswered ARP requests for 10.38.22.40. The DUT tried at
     layer 2 and nothing was there.
   - Poll cadence: four polls 2 s apart at the start, then 64 s steady. That matches
     `Update interval` rising from 2.0 s to 64.6 s.
  => STEP 3 PASS: the wire confirms the client role completely. 7 of 7 polls were
     answered and paired, and the deliberately unreachable server got no NTP packet at
     all. Two product behaviours in these eight lines would mislead a later reader, so
     they are recorded here. First, the DUT uses a DIFFERENT random UDP source port on
     every poll (42599, 36619, 37753, 54407, 50059, 55646, 44203). A capture filter, a
     hardware ACL or a management filter written for "source port 123" will miss every
     packet this client sends. Second, the DUT puts a RANDOM transmit timestamp in each
     request (04:39:39, 21:02:48, 10:31:57, 19:03:59, 16:54:51, 10:15:47, 18:31:21), none
     of them the real time. This is the NTP client privacy behaviour. The exchange still
     works because the server copies the random value back into the origin field, and
     `paired=7` proves that. Anyone who reads that field as "the DUT's clock" will think
     the DUT's clock is wildly and randomly wrong. It is not.

