  -> show running-config | include ntp   : nothing
  -> show ntp status / associations / counters : all three COMPLETELY EMPTY again
awplus(config)#ntp server 10.38.22.33             (04:05:57)

tb277$ tools/tb ckcon ... '@poll 300 Leap\sstatus.*Normal show ntp status'
@poll MATCHED after 1.2 s (1 reads)

Reference ID    : 0A261621 (10.38.22.33)
Stratum         : 6
System time     : 0.000000002 seconds fast of NTP time
Last offset     : -0.000002382 seconds
Leap status     : Normal
* 10.38.22.33   10.32.16.2   5 u  12  64  17  0.564  -0.000  0.003
                                           <-- synchronised about 24 s after the re-add
                                               (04:05:57 -> matched 04:06:21), from a
                                               clock that was 2.12 s out

  The log gives the mechanism, and proves it was a restart:
2026 Oct 21 04:06:07 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[4647]: System clock wrong by 2.122975 seconds
2026 Oct 21 04:06:09 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[4647]: System clock was stepped by 2.122975 seconds
                                           <-- the chronyd PID changed from 3784 to 4647
  => STEP 5 PASS: re-applying `ntp server` restarts chronyd, and the restarted daemon
     stepped the clock in 2 seconds. The size of the step, 2.122975 s, matches the
     association's `offset 2122.966` ms read before the removal, to within 9 microseconds.
     So the mechanism is now pinned: a RUNNING chronyd will not step a clock that
     `clock set` moved under it, but a FRESHLY STARTED one will. That is a product
     behaviour a developer should see, and a practical rule for anyone on this bench.

