  The kiss-o'-death came from the TESTBOX's own ntpd, whose stock configuration carries
  the `limited kod` restriction. The DUT's boot-time burst -- two polls in the same
  millisecond, from two source ports -- tripped the SERVER's rate limiter. The DUT's own
  log agrees:
2026 Oct 21 04:08:50 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[2222]: Received KoD RATE from 10.38.22.33
  The DUT then did the correct thing under the standard: it backed its poll interval off
  to 128 s and stopped using that source (`refid RATE`, `reach 0`, `Stratum 0`,
  `Leap status Not synchronised`).
  => STEP 7 PASS: the saved `ntp server 10.38.22.33` came back by itself after the cold
     boot, and the client resumed polling 9 s after the login prompt with nothing typed
     into the device. That is reboot persistence proved by packets. The kiss-o'-death is
     the test bench server's rate limiter, not a DUT defect, and the DUT answered it
     correctly.

STEP 8 -- time to re-acquire synchronisation from cold, measured not assumed       PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: at 180 s after the boot the DUT was still `Not synchronised`. On its own that
  is ambiguous: it could be an ordinary cold start, a clock too far out to step, or a real
  failure to resume. So it was polled out to a longer window instead of being graded early.

    time (UTC)           state
    ----------           -----
    04:07:42             `reload` issued
    04:08:42             login prompt -- 60 s
    04:08:51             first polls on the wire; one answered, one answered with KoD RATE
    04:09:01 - 04:12:01  `@poll 180 ...` TIMED OUT after 180.6 s (109 reads) -- Stratum 0,
                         Reference ID 00000000, refid RATE, reach 0, Not synchronised
    04:17:29             backoff over, polling again: `10.38.22.33 ... reach 1 ...
                         offset 593.552` ms, still Not synchronised
    04:19:34             `chronyd[2222]: System clock was stepped by 0.592688 seconds`
    04:19:37             `@poll ... MATCHED`: Stratum 6, Reference ID 0A261621
                         (10.38.22.33), Last offset -0.592688382 s, Leap status Normal,
                         `* 10.38.22.33 ... reach 7 ... offset 592.691`
  => STEP 8 PASS: the DUT re-acquired synchronisation 10 min 55 s after the login prompt.
     About nine of those minutes were the kiss-o'-death backoff caused by the SERVER's
     rate limiter, not by the DUT. For comparison, the same DUT against the same server
     with no kiss-o'-death took about 75 s from `ntp server` being applied (step 2), and
     about 24 s on a daemon restart (step 5). It needed four good samples
     (`Client Valid Received 4`, `reach 7`) at a 64 s poll before it would correct the
     clock. The size of the step, 0.592688 s, is also a measurement of this DUT's clock
     drift across a 60 s reboot: the real-time clock keeps time across a power cycle and
     loses roughly half a second doing it.
